CAT = 'ncptPatientPrep'; SEG = 'PRP'
Q = [
("How does a phlebotomist best confirm an outpatient's identity?",
 'Have the patient say name and birth date', ['Read the name aloud and ask for a yes', 'Ask the patient sitting in the next chair', 'Check the name on the clinic list'],
 'Asking the patient to say their own name and date of birth, then matching the order, avoids the "yes" a patient may give to a wrong name.'),
('An inpatient has no ID band on the wrist. What does the phlebotomist do?',
 'Have the nurse band the patient', ['Draw and label from the door card', 'Draw and band the patient later', 'Ask a visitor to confirm the name'],
 'An unbanded inpatient is not drawn; the nursing staff place a correct ID band before the specimen is collected.'),
('How many patient identifiers are matched before a draw?',
 'At least two', ['Exactly one', 'At least four', 'Only the room number'],
 'Two identifiers such as full name and date of birth are matched against the order; a room or bed number is never one of them.'),
('Where is the ID band checked on a newborn in a crib?',
 'On the baby itself', ['On the crib card', "On the mother's wrist", 'On the chart cover'],
 'WHO guidance says the band must be on the patient, not on the cot or crib, since babies can be moved between cribs.'),
('A requisition is missing the test to be drawn. What is the best step?',
 'Clarify the order first', ['Draw a rainbow of every tube', 'Guess from the diagnosis', 'Draw the usual panel and ask later'],
 'An unclear order is clarified with the ordering provider or nurse before collection so the right tubes are drawn.'),
('A fasting glucose is ordered. What should the phlebotomist ask?',
 'When the patient last ate', ['What the patient weighs', 'Which arm the patient writes with', 'When the patient last slept'],
 'Fasting tests need confirmation that the patient has not eaten for the required time; a nonfasting sample is documented or rescheduled as policy says.'),
('A patient has a dialysis fistula in the left arm. Where is blood drawn?',
 'The other arm', ['Just above the fistula', 'Directly from the fistula', 'Just below the fistula'],
 "An arm with a fistula is not used for venipuncture or a tourniquet, because damage could ruin the patient's dialysis access."),
('A patient asks what the draw will feel like. What is the best reply?',
 'Say it is a brief pinch, then explain', ['Promise it will not hurt at all', 'Say nothing and start the draw', 'Tell the patient to look away and relax'],
 'Honest, simple explanation builds trust and cooperation; promising no pain is untrue and harms trust when it hurts.'),
('Why does the phlebotomist look for the vein before the tourniquet goes on?',
 'To keep tourniquet time short', ['To warm the arm first', 'To test for a latex allergy', 'To let the alcohol dry faster'],
 'WHO guidance says to locate the vein first, then apply the tourniquet and re-examine, so the tourniquet is not left on while searching.'),
('What is the longest a tourniquet should stay on before release?',
 '1 minute', ['5 minutes', '10 minutes', '3 minutes'],
 'Standard practice limits tourniquet time to about one minute; longer time causes hemoconcentration and changes test results.'),
('Why is a tourniquet left on too long a problem?',
 'It concentrates the blood', ['It dilutes the plasma', 'It cools down the specimen', 'It adds an anticoagulant'],
 'Stasis pushes fluid out of the vein, so cells, proteins and other analytes become falsely concentrated.'),
('Why is the thumb avoided for an adult fingerstick?',
 'It is often callused', ['It has no capillaries', 'It bleeds too slowly', 'It gives arterial blood'],
 'WHO advises against the thumb and index finger because calluses hinder a good puncture, and against the little finger because its tissue is thin.'),
('In the common H-shaped pattern, which vein crosses the antecubital area?',
 'Median cubital vein', ['Basilic vein', 'Brachial artery', 'Radial vein'],
 'The median cubital links the cephalic and basilic veins across the bend of the elbow, and it is usually the vein chosen for routine draws.'),
('A nurse is starting a new IV. When is it acceptable, though not ideal, to take blood from the new catheter?',
 'Before the IV fluid is connected', ['After the fluid runs an hour', 'Any time the line is open', 'Only from the IV bag port'],
 'WHO guidance says blood may be taken when an in-dwelling device is first placed, before it is connected to fluids, though this is not ideal.'),
('Which capillary puncture site suits an adult?',
 'Middle or ring finger, off-center', ['Center of the thumb pad', 'Side of the little finger', 'Tip of the index finger'],
 'WHO recommends the middle or ring finger; the thumb and index finger have calluses and the little finger has thin tissue.'),
('Up to about what age is the heel the usual capillary site?',
 '6 months', ['12 months', '2 years', '3 weeks'],
 'WHO guidance uses the heel from birth to about 6 months and the finger after that.'),
('Which part of an infant heel is used for capillary puncture?',
 'Medial or lateral plantar side', ['The back curve of the heel', 'The center of the arch', 'The pads of the big toes'],
 'The sides of the plantar surface are used; the back of the heel lies close to the bone and risks osteomyelitis.'),
('A fingerstick is made along the fingerprint lines instead of across them. What happens?',
 'Blood runs down the grooves', ['The puncture goes too deep', 'The first drop is skipped', 'The blood clots faster'],
 'A puncture parallel to the lines lets blood channel away; across the lines it beads up so it can be collected.'),
('Why is the site warmed before a capillary puncture?',
 'To raise blood flow', ['To make the skin numb', 'To sterilize the skin', 'To stop the bleeding'],
 'A warm compress dilates vessels and can increase capillary blood flow several times, so the specimen comes easily.'),
('How long is an alcohol-cleaned site left before the puncture?',
 'Until it is completely dry', ['A count of two seconds', 'Until it feels cool to touch', 'Not at all, puncture wet'],
 'Wet alcohol stings, may hemolyze the specimen and reduces antisepsis; WHO advises about 30 seconds of drying.'),
('How is alcohol applied to the venipuncture site?',
 'From the center outward', ['From the edge inward', 'Back and forth across it', 'One dab at the center'],
 'WHO guidance starts at the puncture site and works outward to cover 2 cm or more, so dirt is not dragged back to the center.'),
('A patient pumps the fist again and again while tubes fill. Which result is most affected?',
 'Potassium', ['Blood type', 'Hemoglobin A1c', 'Prothrombin time'],
 'Repeated fist clenching releases potassium from muscle cells, falsely raising it; one gentle fist is fine.'),
('What does the phlebotomist ask a patient who has fainted during past draws?',
 'To lie down for the draw', ['To sit on a tall stool', 'To stand near the door', 'To skip the draw entirely'],
 'A patient who has fainted before is drawn lying down so a faint does not lead to a fall.'),
('A patient has had mastectomies with lymph node removal on both sides. What is done before drawing from an arm?',
 "Get the provider's permission", ['Use whichever arm looks best', 'Draw from the neck instead', 'Apply the tourniquet tighter'],
 "With node removal on both sides, there is no safe arm by default; facility policy usually needs the provider's permission and guidance before either arm is used."),
('What must be documented on the requisition when a specimen is collected?',
 'Collection date, time, collector', ['The drawing room temperature', "The patient's height and weight", "The patient's home address"],
 "The collection date, time and the collector's initials or ID make the specimen traceable and allow timed tests to be interpreted."),
("Yesterday's draw left a large bruise in the left antecubital area. What is chosen today?",
 'A site away from the bruise', ['The center of the bruise', 'Just below the bruise', 'The same puncture hole'],
 'Blood from a bruised area can give wrong results and the draw is more painful, so another site is used.'),
('A patient fasting for a morning glucose test asks what they may drink. What is the usual instruction?',
 'Plain water only', ['Coffee with milk', 'Fruit juice in small sips', 'A sports drink'],
 'Fasting usually means no food or drink except water; milky coffee, juice and sports drinks can change glucose and other results. The lab or provider gives the exact fasting time.'),
('When should a tourniquet be released during the draw?',
 'When blood flows, before withdrawal', ['Right after the needle is withdrawn', 'Before the needle enters the vein', 'Only after the bandage is on'],
 'The tourniquet is released when blood flow is established and always before the needle is withdrawn, or a hematoma can form.'),
]
